**Current versus accepted.** Alpha HTML entries still come from root activity. The accepted design requires a root purpose, initially `$page`. Dependencies never activate their roots.

### Root activation

Top-level runtime code in a selected module root becomes a dormant `start`. It
runs once when that module becomes a page entry. Helper files provide
declarations and never own an entry or an independent start.

### Purpose and comments

An unmarked root with authored runtime work or direct output needs a purpose,
even inside `if false`. API-only declarations need no purpose. Semantically
classified `$doc`, `$note` and `$todo` templates count as documentation and
don't need one.

An accepted page can contain metadata alone:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    $page
]

It still emits a complete document. A dependency cannot make unpurposed
runtime work legal by keeping it dormant.

### Page fragments

Direct top-level templates in the selected root remain fragments:

- A const fragment (the `#` prefix form) becomes static HTML.
- A runtime fragment (the plain bracket form) runs through the root's `start`.

The builder keeps static and runtime fragments in source order. Assigned or
returned templates are ordinary values, and helpers cannot create page
fragments by themselves.
